  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\n**** Mid-Week Rainfall ****\n\nAs a short-wave trough evident on 08z/2am water vapor imagery\nover the Pacific Northwest tracks eastward and phases with a\nsecond wave dropping out of northern Saskatchewan/Manitoba, a\nprominent upper trough will form over the Great Lakes/Ohio River\nValley by the end of the week. As the phasing process commences,\na surface low will gradually deepen from 1014mb over South\nDakota this evening to 1005mb over Indiana by Wednesday \nevening. The low is progged to track across the heart of central\nIllinois on Wednesday: however, the areal coverage of its \nassociated precipitation remains somewhat in question. The 00z \nNov 10 suite of models (including CAMs such as the HRRR/RRFS) \nsuggests the strongest lift/deepest moisture may not coincide \nuntil the system is largely to the east of the KILX CWA late \nWednesday. While most models show a few light showers earlier in\nthe day mainly north of the I-72 corridor, high-res solutions \nare not showing any appreciable convection forming ahead of the \nlow until late afternoon/evening...mainly along and east of the \nWabash River in Indiana. While the 00z RRFS indicates \nprecipitable water values increasing to 0.75-1.00 and SBCAPEs \nreaching 500-1000J/kg ahead of the low Wednesday afternoon, it \ndoes not fire convection until close to the Indiana border. The\nlatest Day 2 SPC Convective Outlook has adjusted for this \nchange as well...with the Marginal Risk for severe now focused \njust S/SE across southwestern Indiana and western Kentucky. As a\nresult of this slower development of precip, expected rainfall \nacross central Illinois has decreased. The latest LREF and NBM \nnow only feature a 15-25% chance of more than 0.25...mainly \nfocused along/northeast of a Bloomington to Paris line.\n\n**** End of Week Cool-Down ****\n\nOnce the system departs, the primary forcing for additional\nprecip will shift E/NE of central Illinois Wednesday night\nthrough Thursday night as attention turns to lake-enhanced bands\nof showers from Lake Michigan. The bulk of these showers will\nremain to the NE, but could potentially clip areas\nalong/northeast of a Champaign to Paris line on Thursday where\n20-30 PoPs are warranted. Further west across the remainder of\ncentral Illinois, cannot rule out a few sprinkles due to the\nincreasingly cyclonic flow aloft...but significant precip is not\nexpected. The main weather story on Thursday will be the windy\nand sharply cooler conditions. High temperatures will only reach\nthe middle to upper 40s across much of the region...with lower\n50s south of I-70. Northwesterly winds gusting 20-25mph will\nmake it feel even cooler as wind-chill values hover in the 30s\nfor much of the day. The coolest day of the forecast period will\nbe Friday when highs remain well below normal for this time of\nyear in the lower to middle 40s. As high pressure builds into\nthe region, clear skies and diminishing winds will set up the\ncoldest night of the fall thus far Friday night as lows plummet\ninto the lower to middle 20s. After that, the deep upper trough\nwill gradually pivot E/NE by early next week. As a result,\ntemperatures will slowly rise back to seasonal values in the\nmiddle to upper 50s by next Monday/Tuesday.",
